KG42.register("rules-06102c2593239ea76abff60a",{"id":"09-local_flexibility::v141-c-R004","paper":"09-local_flexibility","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R004","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Local clearing price","process_stage":"pricing","market_meaning":"For conditional flexibility services, local market clearing determines the final activation cost. Scope: EcoGrid 2.0 local flexibility market for DSO services. Condition: The acquired flexibility service is conditional.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function pricing.local under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R004-E001","source":"clearing.local","target":"pricing.local","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R004-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e26","v141-b-g03-e13"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"EcoGrid 2.0 local flexibility market for DSO services.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e26","v141-b-g03-e13"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"For conditional flexibility services, local market clearing determines the final activation cost. Scope: EcoGrid 2.0 local flexibility market for DSO services. Condition: The acquired flexibility service is conditional.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e26","v141-b-g03-e13"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e26","location":{"location_id":"src-65c3762894-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.4 Activation benefit evaluation for conditional services","anchor_text":"In the case of conditional services, the DSO has to pay an activationng fee to the aggregators. The ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In the case of conditional services, the DSO has to pay an activation fee to the aggregators. The DSO has to make the decision whether it is beneficial to activate the service or not. Activation costs are specified by the aggregators as part of their bids, hence the final activation cost is determined by the outcome of the market clearing. The service activation signal can be sent up to one hour before the service delivery period. At this point, the uncertainty of load forecasts is smaller, as the delivery period is closer.\nTo estimate the benefit of service activation, load samples are generated in the same manner as before. However, at this point lead time is much smaller, such that the generated load samples have a much smaller uncertainty compared to the network operational planning stage. The DSO can decide whether or not to activate a service by comparing the expected benefit of the service with the activation cost. The risk of the DSO deviating from this specified activation probability, by activating conditional services more or less often, is carried by the aggregators. Thus, aggregators have the incentive to specify their real reserve cost, as well as their real service activation cost in their bids."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-65c3762894-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.4 Activation benefit evaluation for conditional services","anchor_text":"In the case of conditional services, the DSO has to pay an activationng fee to the aggregators. The ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Activation costs are specified by the aggregators as part of their bids, hence the final activation cost is determined by the outcome of the market clearing. ... The risk of the DSO deviating from this specified activation probability, by activating conditional services more or less often, is carried by the aggregators. Thus, aggregators have the incentive to specify their real reserve cost, as well as their real service activation cost in their bids."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":"The rule's candidate evidence does not introduce an optimization or operational decision variable for this rule; it states only that a cost outcome is set by market clearing. No symbol, domain, unit, or decision role is defined for a 'final activation cost' entity within this rule's scope, so x remains empty to avoid inventing a variable.","empty_theta_reason":"The rule's candidate evidence does not define fixed constants, coefficients, or parameters within this rule's scope. Aggregator bid costs and reserve/activation costs appear only as qualitative bid content in v141-b-g03-e13, without symbols, values, domains, or units tied to this rule, so theta remains empty."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-activation","v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-settlement"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e26","quote":"Activation costs are specified by the aggregators as part of their bids, hence the final activation cost is determined by the outcome of the market clearing."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e13","quote":"Activation costs are specified by the aggregators as part of their bids, hence the final activation cost is determined by the outcome of the market clearing."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"f1f0018188ee2faa8fae180b50b9a397b5e889036db818164ebe11b33ceea562","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"The supplied candidate evidence for this rule (v141-b-g02-e26 and v141-b-g03-e13) states a qualitative market-design dependency: aggregators specify activation costs in their bids, and the final activation cost is determined by the outcome of the market clearing. It provides no equation, symbol definition, domain, unit, coefficient, or optimization/decision variable for this rule, and C.1 already marked C001 procedural with expression=null. Therefore the rule is retained as a procedural dependency between the clearing and pricing nodes rather than formalized into an invented mathematical expression.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","node_ids":["clearing.local","pricing.local"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001"],"rationale":"The rule's frozen edge E001 links clearing.local to pricing.local, and the constraint's members are exactly those edge endpoints. The source says the final activation cost is determined by the outcome of the market clearing, so the binding records that procedural relation without asserting an unsupported mathematical functional form."}],"revision_requests":["If a formal activation-cost or clearing-price equation is required, B re-extraction should locate the pricing/settlement expression for conditional services. The current evidence only states qualitatively that the final activation cost is determined by market clearing, without symbol definitions, domains, units, or a functional form for this rule."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
